1
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,830
On Saturday, 13 July 1985, at 6:41 in the evening,

2
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:18,950
Freddie Mercury walked onto the stage at Wembley Stadium in London,

3
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:21,490
in front of about 72,000 people.

4
00:00:22,023 --> 00:00:24,938
He was thirty-eight years old, and he had twenty-one minutes.

5
00:00:25,547 --> 00:00:28,362
Six years and four months later, he would be gone.

6
00:00:28,917 --> 00:00:32,952
This is the story of those twenty-one minutes, and of what came after them.

7
00:00:42,133 --> 00:00:48,503
He was born Farrokh Bulsara in Zanzibar on 5 September 1946,

8
00:00:48,993 --> 00:00:52,263
and at the age of eight he was sent to a boarding school in India.

9
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:57,330
In 1964 his family fled the revolution in Zanzibar and settled near London,

10
00:00:57,660 --> 00:01:01,290
and in 1970 he formed Queen with Brian May and Roger Taylor.

11
00:01:01,833 --> 00:01:05,663
By 1985 Queen was among the best-known bands in the world,

12
00:01:05,933 --> 00:01:09,443
but its name had recently been in the newspapers for the wrong reason.

13
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,990
In October 1984 the band had played a run of concerts at Sun City,

14
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:16,770
in South Africa, during the years of apartheid.

15
00:01:17,462 --> 00:01:19,457
The British Musicians' Union fined them,

16
00:01:19,707 --> 00:01:21,797
the United Nations put them on its blacklist,

17
00:01:22,267 --> 00:01:25,917
and in 2021 Roger Taylor called the trip a kind of mistake.

18
00:01:26,511 --> 00:01:30,186
Then came Live Aid, a concert for famine relief in Ethiopia,

19
00:01:30,576 --> 00:01:33,226
organised by Bob Geldof and Midge Ure,

20
00:01:33,616 --> 00:01:36,146
and played on one day in London and in Philadelphia.

21
00:01:36,867 --> 00:01:39,097
About 72,000 people filled Wembley

22
00:01:39,107 --> 00:01:42,477
and about 89,000 filled the stadium in Philadelphia,

23
00:01:42,987 --> 00:01:45,957
and the two shows were watched on television around the world.

24
00:01:46,567 --> 00:01:50,357
The promoter Harvey Goldsmith has said that Geldof doubted Queen at first,

25
00:01:50,787 --> 00:01:52,977
and that he had to dig in to get them on the bill.

26
00:01:53,511 --> 00:01:57,266
Mercury himself worried that playing might look like a political statement,

27
00:01:57,516 --> 00:02:00,306
and he waited to see how the public reacted before he agreed.

28
00:02:00,892 --> 00:02:02,582
And the band itself was not sure.

29
00:02:11,367 --> 00:02:13,517
Roger Taylor was the keenest of the four.

30
00:02:14,367 --> 00:02:16,717
Brian May has said that once Mercury dug in,

31
00:02:16,867 --> 00:02:20,397
he would not budge, until May told him they would regret staying away.

32
00:02:20,942 --> 00:02:23,392
The band rehearsed at the Shaw Theatre in London,

33
00:02:23,842 --> 00:02:28,052
because with so little time on stage, every minute had to count.

34
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:47,770
Queen's slot came in the early evening, between Dire Straits and David Bowie.

35
00:02:48,350 --> 00:02:50,105
Mercury sat at a grand piano

36
00:02:50,115 --> 00:02:53,105
and opened with a short version of Bohemian Rhapsody.

37
00:02:53,602 --> 00:02:55,012
Then the band hit Radio Ga Ga,

38
00:02:55,302 --> 00:02:57,952
and thousands of arms rose and clapped in time with him.

39
00:03:07,428 --> 00:03:11,823
One sustained note became known as The Note Heard Round the World.

40
00:03:12,403 --> 00:03:15,738
He led them through Hammer to Fall and Crazy Little Thing Called Love.

41
00:03:26,938 --> 00:03:30,743
The set ended with a short We Will Rock You and then We Are the Champions,

42
00:03:31,253 --> 00:03:32,563
and then it was over.

43
00:03:33,133 --> 00:03:37,563
Bob Geldof said later that Queen were absolutely the best band of the day.

44
00:03:51,028 --> 00:03:54,843
In 2005 a poll of more than sixty artists,

45
00:03:55,073 --> 00:03:55,443
journalists

46
00:03:55,453 --> 00:04:00,363
and music executives named it the greatest live rock performance of all time.

47
00:04:00,841 --> 00:04:06,176
Live Aid is estimated to have raised about 150 million pounds for famine relief.

48
00:04:06,620 --> 00:04:09,790
Estimates of the television audience for the whole day vary widely,

49
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:12,890
from several hundred million to nearly two billion people.

50
00:04:13,393 --> 00:04:18,188
Classic Rock magazine has called it arguably the most iconic moment of the band's career.

51
00:04:23,662 --> 00:04:27,517
On 9 August 1986, at Knebworth Park in England,

52
00:04:28,147 --> 00:04:31,017
Queen played to more than 120,000 people.

53
00:04:31,524 --> 00:04:34,639
It was the last concert Queen ever played with Freddie Mercury.

54
00:04:35,250 --> 00:04:38,880
Two months later, British newspapers began to speculate about his health,

55
00:04:39,210 --> 00:04:41,100
and Mercury said he was perfectly fit.

56
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:45,690
He kept working. In 1987 he released a duet, Barcelona,

57
00:04:46,140 --> 00:04:51,330
with the Spanish opera singer Montserrat Caballe, and an album followed in 1988.

58
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:04,550
He chose to keep his illness private, and he went on working:

59
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:07,150
giving interviews, recording, filming.

60
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:10,430
He told almost nobody outside a small circle,

61
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:13,510
and in public he never confirmed it until the end.

62
00:05:21,043 --> 00:05:26,123
From 1989 Queen recorded a new album, Innuendo, partly in Montreux,

63
00:05:26,313 --> 00:05:30,303
in Switzerland, because reporters kept bothering Mercury at his home in London.

64
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:34,910
On 18 February 1990, at the Dominion Theatre in London,

65
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:39,170
Queen collected the Brit Award for Outstanding Contribution to British Music.

66
00:05:49,362 --> 00:05:51,037
It was his last public appearance.

67
00:05:56,695 --> 00:06:01,070
Innuendo came out in the United Kingdom on 4 February 1991,

68
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:02,490
and it reached number one.

69
00:06:03,029 --> 00:06:08,939
On 30 May 1991 he filmed the video for These Are the Days of Our Lives.

70
00:06:09,949 --> 00:06:12,519
It was shot in colour, and released in black and white.

71
00:06:13,138 --> 00:06:18,813
In June 1991 he made his last recordings, at Mountain Studios in Montreux.

72
00:06:29,167 --> 00:06:33,697
On 22 November 1991 he asked the band's manager,

73
00:06:33,987 --> 00:06:35,817
Jim Beach, to prepare a statement.

74
00:06:36,462 --> 00:06:41,717
It was released on 23 November. In it, Mercury confirmed that he had AIDS,

75
00:06:42,187 --> 00:06:44,897
said he had kept it private to protect the people around him,

76
00:06:45,147 --> 00:06:47,817
and asked the world to join the fight against the disease.

77
00:06:48,567 --> 00:06:52,017
He died the next day, 24 November 1991,

78
00:06:52,447 --> 00:06:56,377
at his home in Kensington, London. He was forty-five years old.

79
00:06:56,873 --> 00:06:59,943
The official cause was bronchial pneumonia, brought on by AIDS.

80
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:11,970
His funeral, on 27 November, was private, and a Zoroastrian priest led it.

81
00:07:12,328 --> 00:07:18,643
On 20 April 1992, about 72,000 people filled Wembley Stadium again,

82
00:07:18,933 --> 00:07:22,823
for a tribute concert organised by the three surviving members of Queen.

83
00:07:23,427 --> 00:07:26,002
Elizabeth Taylor spoke by satellite about AIDS,

84
00:07:26,572 --> 00:07:29,562
and an estimated one billion people watched on television.

85
00:07:40,233 --> 00:07:43,023
The money raised launched the Mercury Phoenix Trust,

86
00:07:43,393 --> 00:07:45,523
which supports AIDS research and charities.

87
00:07:46,167 --> 00:07:49,457
It was the same stadium, and about the same number of people.

88
00:07:50,207 --> 00:07:54,917
In 1985 they sang back to him, and in 1992 they gathered for him.

89
00:07:55,517 --> 00:07:59,987
The world remembers twenty-one minutes at Wembley. What do you remember?

